# Host-side ports for the local dev stack (docker-compose.dev.yml + Makefile).
# Copy this file to .env and override whatever clashes with other work on
# your machine — see README.md "Ports" for a worked example. Only the host
# side of each mapping is configurable; container-internal ports (and the
# four zone-app dev servers, 4301-4304) are fixed.
#
# Every value below is the current default — an empty/missing .env behaves
# exactly like today.

HELIVANTA_PG_PORT=5432
HELIVANTA_NATS_PORT=4222
HELIVANTA_NATS_MONITOR_PORT=8222
HELIVANTA_REDIS_PORT=6379
HELIVANTA_OPENFGA_PORT=8090
HELIVANTA_ZITADEL_PORT=20080
HELIVANTA_ZITADEL_PG_PORT=5433
HELIVANTA_API_PORT=8080

# HOSTNAMES, not ports — and unlike everything above, these are not just a
# collision escape hatch (#916, design spec D6). The stack serves the app at
# helivanta.localhost and Zitadel at auth.tesserix.localhost, on purpose:
# production serves them on different registrable domains (helivanta.app vs
# auth.tesserix.app), which makes every browser request from the app to the
# IdP CROSS-SITE, and a SameSite=Lax IdP cookie is withheld on those.
#
# The old dev setup put both on `localhost` and differed only by port. A
# "site" is scheme + registrable domain and PORTS ARE NOT PART OF IT, so dev
# was same-site: the cross-site defect #916 turned out to be worked perfectly
# here and no test could have failed. helivanta.localhost and
# tesserix.localhost are distinct registrable domains that Chrome and macOS
# still resolve to loopback with no /etc/hosts edit, so dev now reproduces
# production's relationship. e2e/tests/cross-site-harness.spec.ts proves it
# against a real browser.
#
# Override these only if the names collide with something on your machine.
# Every consumer reads these variables rather than a literal — the compose
# file, scripts/zitadel-bootstrap.mjs, the API's whole Zitadel configuration
# (the Makefile derives and exports ZITADEL_ISSUER_URL,
# ZITADEL_HOSTED_LOGIN_URL and HELIVANTA_WEB_ORIGIN from them, so the
# hardcoded dev defaults in backend/internal/config/ never answer for a
# stale host), scripts/e2e.sh, scripts/lib/zitadel.mjs's dev redirect URIs,
# and the e2e suite via e2e/tests/support/hosts.ts — which loads THIS file
# itself (load-env.ts), so the cross-site guard sees an override even on the
# Make-less `pnpm --filter e2e exec playwright test` path, where nothing else
# would have read it — so an override is honoured end to end, and
# that was verified by re-provisioning the stack on a third hostname and
# running the full e2e suite against it.
#
# What you may NOT do is point both at the same registrable domain: that
# returns the harness to same-site and makes #916's whole class of defect
# invisible again. cross-site-harness.spec.ts reads the live configuration
# and fails if you try, so this is a control rather than a request.
#
# Changing HELIVANTA_ZITADEL_HOST on an already-provisioned stack requires
# `RESET_YES=1 make reset`: Zitadel writes its instance domain once, at
# first-instance time, and answers "Instance not found" to any other host
# afterwards. scripts/preflight.sh checks for exactly that and says so.
# HELIVANTA_WEB_HOST=helivanta.localhost
# HELIVANTA_ZITADEL_HOST=auth.tesserix.localhost

# ZITADEL_LOGIN_CLIENT_TOKEN is NOT a port and normally does not belong in
# this file — `make dev-api` reads it straight from
# dev/zitadel/secrets/login-client.pat (written by Zitadel's own
# first-instance provisioning) and needs no override on a normal clone.
# Set it here ONLY if you are pointing dev-api at a login-client PAT that
# did not come from this machine's own dev/zitadel/secrets/ (e.g. a
# shared remote Zitadel). Never commit a real value — this repo is public
# to anyone who can read the source, and this PAT can finalise an OIDC
# auth request for ANY app on the Zitadel instance it targets (see
# backend/internal/config/config.go's ZitadelLoginClientToken doc comment).
# ZITADEL_LOGIN_CLIENT_TOKEN=

# HELIVANTA_WEB_ORIGIN is also not a port. It is the origin ZITADEL_HOSTED_LOGIN_URL
# is checked against at boot (backend/internal/config/hostedlogin.go),
# so a misconfigured hosted-login URL can never loop an MFA-enrolled
# clinician back to Helivanta's own login page. Defaults to
# http://helivanta.localhost:4301 in dev (HELIVANTA_ENV=dev) and needs no override on a
# normal clone. OUTSIDE dev the API refuses to boot without this set —
# it is a NEW PRODUCTION PREREQUISITE, alongside ZITADEL_LOGIN_CLIENT_TOKEN,
# for whatever deploys Helivanta's API in a real environment (that wiring lives
# in tesserix-infra, not this repo).
# HELIVANTA_WEB_ORIGIN=

# TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS is a comma-separated list of CIDR blocks whose IMMEDIATE
# TCP peer this API trusts to have set X-Forwarded-For honestly. It is what makes
# every IP-keyed rate limit in this service real rather than decorative: without
# it, gin trusts ALL proxies by default, so a caller-supplied X-Forwarded-For
# yields a fresh full-burst bucket per request and the login/factor limits are
# bypassable with a header (#867, proven by
# TestFactorRateLimitNotBypassableBySpoofedXForwardedFor).
#
# MANDATORY OUTSIDE DEV (#824 Task 1): the API now REFUSES TO BOOT if this is
# unset or empty and HELIVANTA_ENV is not "dev" — config.RequireTrustedProxyCIDRs,
# called from cmd/api/main.go alongside the other boot guards. An unset value
# would otherwise leave gin trusting the raw TCP peer only, which collapses every
# caller behind a shared ingress into ONE rate-limit bucket, so
# RATE_LIMIT_LOGIN_PER_MIN becomes a hospital-wide budget any single caller can
# exhaust. That must be a deliberate choice, not an unconfigured default.
#
# If a deployment genuinely has no proxy in front of it, set TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS=none
# — the ONLY accepted opt-out (not "off", not "false"). It preserves the
# raw-TCP-peer behaviour explicitly, without forcing you to invent a CIDR that
# misdescribes your network. Leave it unset in dev only; set it (to a real CIDR
# list or to "none") everywhere else.
#
# PRODUCTION VALUE, for the cluster this deploys to today: 10.20.0.0/16 — the
# GKE pod CIDR of tesseract-prod-in-gke (confirmed via
# `gcloud container clusters describe tesseract-prod-in-gke --region=asia-south1`).
# The request path is
#   browser -> Cloudflare edge -> cloudflared POD -> istio-ingressgateway -> this API
# so BOTH hops in front of this API are in-cluster pods. The main gateway's
# xff-trust-hops EnvoyFilter runs use_remote_address with xff_num_trusted_hops: 1
# and appends, so trusting the pod CIDR lets gin walk X-Forwarded-For from the
# RIGHT past the in-cluster hop and land on the real client.
#
# Do NOT copy go-shared's approach. Its getClientIP prefers X-Real-Client-IP from
# the add-client-ip-header EnvoyFilter, which (a) targets custom-ingressgateway,
# not the gateway this API sits behind, (b) carries downstreamDirectRemoteAddress
# — a POD IP, not a client — and (c) is added rather than replaced, so a
# client-supplied value survives; its fallback then takes X-Forwarded-For's
# LEFTMOST entry, which is fully attacker-controlled.
#
# Bare IPs are rejected: entries are parsed with net.ParseCIDR, so use 10.0.0.5/32
# rather than 10.0.0.5. Unparseable entries are warn-logged and dropped, which
# fails closed to trusting no proxy.
#
# NOT YET CONFIRMED against a live pod: no Helivanta pod is deployed (#824 owns
# the image and chart), so the exact X-Forwarded-For string arriving here has not
# been observed. The value above follows from the verified Envoy config and gin's
# documented right-to-left walk. #824 should confirm it with a real request.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS=10.20.0.0/16
